<html>
<title>How do I install Elf/Os 2.8 on an Elf?</title>
<body>

<h1>How do I install Elf/Os 2.8 on an Elf?</h1>

<p>
Elf/Os is a disk-based operating system for Elf-class computers designed by Mike Riley. See the <a href="http://www.elf-emulation.com">Elf/1802 Emulation</a> website.
</p>

<p>
During installation the following <tt>.ide</tt> files are provided with Elf/Os 2.8 already installed:
</p>

<ul>
<li>pixie-installation.ide</li>
<li>mc6845-installation.ide</li>
<li>mc6847-installation.ide</li>
<li>tms-installation.ide</li>
<li>gra-installation.ide</li>
<li>serial-installation.ide</li>
<li>vt100-installation.ide</li>
</ul>

<p>
These files include a pre-installed Elf/Os system and additional Elf/Os software. The installation files will be renamed to pixie.ide, mc6845.ide, mc6847.ide, tms.ide, gra.ide, serial.ide, or vt100.ide if the used file doesn't exist.
</p>

<p>
The following example will install Elf/Os 2.8 via a VT100 terminal. To change video output after installation, follow the FAQ <a href="FaqElfInstBiosio.htm">How do I install Elf/Os 2.5 via Biosio?</a> afterwards. 
</p>

<p>
To start installation, first load the XML configuration file <tt>elfos-installation-serial.xml</tt> located under the Elf computer. Now follow these steps:
</p>

<h2>1. Press <strong>Start</strong></h2>

<p>
This should start one of the Elf computer windows and a VT100 window. Press the return key in the VT100 window. This will set the baud rate and then display the following menu:
</p>

<pre>
   Elf/Os Installation
   1> Run hard drive init tool
   2> Run filesystem gen tool
   3> Run sys tool
   4> Install binaries
   5> Boot Elf/OS

      Option ?
</pre>

<h2>2. Select option 1> Run hard drive init tool</h2>

<p>
Press <strong>Q</strong> for a quick format, which initializes the hard drive. 
</p>

<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>

<pre>
   Total Sectors: 53248
   Format Complete
</pre>

<p>
After this the menu will be redrawn. 
</p>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> This step only needs to be performed once. If you have already run the hard drive init tool on your emulated drives, you will never need to run it again. </p>

<h2>3. Select option 2> Run filesystem gen tool</h2>

<p>
This will set up the filesystem on the disk. 
</p>

<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>

<pre>
  IDE File System Gen Utility

  Total Sectors: 53248
  AU Size: 8
  Total AUs: 6656
  Master Dir Sector: 48
  Filesystem generation complete
</pre>

<p>
After which you again get the installation menu.
</p>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> This step normally only needs to be run once. You would only need to run this option again if you desire to wipe out the contents of your drive.</p>

<h2>4. Select option 3> Run sys tool</h2>

<p>
This will copy the OS kernel to the hard disk. 
</p>

<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>

<pre>
  System copied.
</pre>

<h2>5. Select option 4> Install Binaries</h2>

<p>
This will install the binary utilities onto the disk. 
</p>

<ul>
<li><tt>DIR</tt> - View the disk directory</li>
<li><tt>SAVE</tt> - Save memory contents to an executable file</li>
<li><tt>DUMP</tt> - Dump memory contents to executable files</li>
<li><tt>MINIMON</tt> - Simple monitor for viewing and modifying memory</li>
<li><tt>LOAD</tt> - Load an executable program into memory without executing it</li>
<li><tt>FREE</tt> - Show disk usage statistics</li>
<li><tt>DEL</tt> - Delete files</li>
<li><tt>HEXDUMP</tt> - Display the contents of a file in hex</li>
<li><tt>COPY</tt> - Copy files</li>
<li><tt>RENAME</tt> - Rename files</li>
<li><tt>TYPE</tt> - Display the contents of an ASCII file</li>
<li><tt>EDIT</tt> - Simple text editor</li>
<li><tt>EXEC</tt> - Execute a program already in memory</li>
<li><tt>RINSTALL</tt> - Load additional packages</li>
<li><tt>MKDIR</tt> - Make a directory</li>
<li><tt>RMDIR</tt> - Remove a directory</li>
<li><tt>CHDIR</tt> - Change or view the current directory</li>
<li><tt>PATCH</tt> - Apply program patches</li>
<li><tt>VER</tt> - Get the Elf/Os version</li>
<li><tt>ASM</tt> - Assembler</li>
<li><tt>STAT</tt> - View file statistics</li>
<li><tt>SEDIT</tt></li>
</ul>


<h2>6. Select option 5> Boot Elf/Os</h2>

<p>
This will boot Elf/Os. 
</p>

<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>

<pre>
  Elf/OS Ready
</pre>

<h2>7. Start Elf/Os</h2>

<p>
From now on you can start Elf/Os directly by loading XML configuration <tt>elfos-serial.xml</tt>.
</p>

</body>
</html>
